Database migrations for the SmileAI portal.

init_db() in db.py runs `alembic upgrade head` on every backend start, so a
deploy needs no separate migration step -- restarting the app is enough.

Revisions
---------
2a9de32687b8  baseline schema -- every table as it stood when Alembic was added
1d1fe49baabb  the FKs and indexes the old ALTER TABLE path could never create

Adding a migration
------------------
Edit the models in db.py, then:

    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "what changed"

Read the generated file before committing it. Autogenerate cannot see a rename:
it emits a drop plus an add, which throws away the column's data. Rewrite those
as op.alter_column(..., new_column_name=...) by hand.

Existing databases
------------------
A database created before Alembic existed has no alembic_version row.
_stamp_legacy_database() in db.py detects that, stamps it at the baseline, and
lets only the later revisions run -- nothing is rebuilt. This happens
automatically; there is no manual step for the AWS deploy.

SQLite vs Postgres
------------------
env.py sets render_as_batch for SQLite because SQLite cannot ALTER a column in
place; batch mode rebuilds the table by copy-and-swap. Postgres runs the
statements directly. Name every constraint explicitly -- an unnamed one works
under batch mode and then fails on RDS.

Useful commands
---------------
    alembic current              # where this database is
    alembic history --verbose    # the whole chain
    alembic check                # models vs database: undeclared drift?
    alembic downgrade -1         # step back one revision
    alembic upgrade head --sql   # print SQL instead of applying it
